// bsearch: BINARY SEARCH ON A SORTED TABLE, ITS ANSWER PROVEN BOTH WAYS.
//
// WHAT IT IS. On a sorted table a, ∀ i ≤ j : a[i] ≤ a[j], binary search
// (Bentley, Programming Pearls; Knuth vol. 3 §6.2.1) halves the interval
// [lo, hi) that may still hold t. Its invariant is what it has ruled out:
//     ∀ k ∈ [0, lo) : a[k] < t   and   ∀ k ∈ [hi, n) : t < a[k].
//   - a step to the right, a[mid] < t, rules out [lo, mid]: for k there,
//     a[k] ≤ a[mid] < t, an instance of sortedness at (k, mid);
//   - a step to the left, a[mid] > t, rules out [mid, hi) the same way;
//   - at the exit lo ≥ hi, the two ruled-out parts cover the table: t is absent.
// The claim is both directions: a found index holds t, and −1 means no index
// does. Each sequent's universal hypotheses, sortedness and the invariant, are
// instantiated over the index set (ADR 0145 stage 3), the midpoint
// lo + (hi − lo)/2 read exactly (Bloch's form, examples/logic/midpoint.oro).
//
// THE MIDPOINT IS A LOOP VARIABLE, m = lo + (hi − lo)/2 in the invariant,
// computed at each `again` under its clause's guards. Written in the guards,
// the midpoint occurs several times and reduction binds it once, before any
// guard, where the decision of each operation inside the word knows nothing
// of lo < hi (it reads intervals, not invariants). The first clause's tests
// past lo >= hi, lo < 0, hi > len(a), m < lo and m >= hi, negate the
// invariant, so the logic proves they never hold; they give the intervals
// what the invariant gives the logic.
//
// The program searches {1, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21} for 8, 1, 21 and 4: 3 0 5 −1.
use io

fn search(a ∈ Array(Int), t ∈ Int) (r ∈ Int)
    where len(a) <= 1000000 && (∀ i ∈ 0..len(a)-1 : ∀ j ∈ 0..len(a)-1 : i <= j ⇒ a[i] <= a[j])
    ensure (r >= 0 ⇒ r < len(a) && a[r] == t) && (r < 0 ⇒ ∀ k ∈ 0..len(a)-1 : a[k] != t)
{
    loop lo = 0, hi = len(a), m = len(a) / 2
        invariant 0 <= lo && lo <= hi && hi <= len(a) && m == lo + (hi - lo) / 2 &&
            (∀ k ∈ 0..lo-1 : a[k] < t) && (∀ k ∈ hi..len(a)-1 : t < a[k])
    {
        lo < 0 || lo >= hi || hi > len(a) || m < lo || m >= hi -> -1
        a[m] < t -> again(m + 1, hi, m + 1 + (hi - (m + 1)) / 2)
        a[m] > t -> again(lo, m, lo + (m - lo) / 2)
        else -> m
    }
}

export fn main() {
    a = array { 1, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21 }
    io.print_int(search(a, 8))
    io.print_int(search(a, 1))
    io.print_int(search(a, 21))
    io.print_int(search(a, 4))
}
